{#
  T-6.05 — the order timeline.

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.ui.order-timeline' with {
        title: trans('theme.orders.tracking_title'),
        steps: [
            { label: 'تم قبول الطلب', date: '2025-02-13', state: 'done' },
            { label: 'جاري شحنه لك',  date: '2025-02-28', state: 'current' },
            { label: 'تم التوصيل',    state: 'upcoming' }
        ]
    } only %}

  Parameters:
    title  optional heading above the list
    steps  required — an ordered list of { label, date?, state }.
           state is 'done' · 'current' · 'upcoming'. **The caller decides**; this
           component derives nothing, because which statuses a store has and
           which one an order has reached are facts only the caller can know.
    level  heading level for `title`, 2 by default

  «EVALUATE `salla-order-shipments` FIRST AND RECORD THE FINDING» — DONE, AND IT
  IS NOT THIS. That component renders a **split-shipment card list**: a carrier
  logo, «الشحنة (1)», a tracking number with a copy button, and product
  thumbnails. It has no steps, no progress and no dates. It also renders
  **nothing at all** unless the store has the `thankyou-page-multi-shipments`
  feature *and* the order has two or more shipments — `render()` returns an empty
  host below that threshold. There is nothing in it a stylesheet could turn into
  a timeline, so technique C was evaluated and found inapplicable rather than
  skipped. **This also corrects the B6 ruling of 2026-08-05**, which named
  `salla-order-shipments` as the source for order tracking: it is the right
  component for split shipments, and the wrong one for progress. Recorded.

  IT IS AN ORDERED LIST AND THAT IS THE POINT. `<ol>` says «these have a
  sequence» to a screen reader without a word of extra markup, which is exactly
  what a timeline means. The current step carries `aria-current="step"` — the
  attribute for this and not a class the assistive layer cannot see.

  PROGRESS IS NEVER CONVEYED BY COLOUR ALONE, AND IT IS CARRIED THREE WAYS.
  A done step is a **filled** dot, an upcoming one is **hollow** — a difference in
  shape that survives greyscale, a monochrome display and any form of colour
  blindness. The current step is larger and ringed. And every step states its own
  condition in `sr-only` text, so the sequence is legible with no visual channel
  at all. WCAG 1.4.1 asks for one alternative to colour; this has two.
#}
{% set timeline_level = level|default(2) %}

<div class="order-timeline">
    {% if title is defined and title is not empty %}
        <h{{ timeline_level }} class="order-timeline__title">{{ title }}</h{{ timeline_level }}>
    {% endif %}

    <ol class="order-timeline__list">
        {% for step in steps %}
            {% set step_state = step.state|default('upcoming') %}

            <li class="order-timeline__step order-timeline__step--{{ step_state }}"
                {% if step_state == 'current' %}aria-current="step"{% endif %}>
                {#
                  The mark is drawn, not lettered: a decorative span the
                  stylesheet turns into a dot and a rail. It is `aria-hidden`
                  because the state beside it is already announced in words.
                #}
                <span class="order-timeline__mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>

                <div class="order-timeline__content">
                    <p class="order-timeline__label">
                        {{ step.label }}
                        <span class="sr-only">
                            — {{ trans('theme.orders.step_' ~ step_state) }}
                        </span>
                    </p>

                    {#
                      A step with no date is not an error — «تم التقييم» has none
                      until it happens, and the artboard draws it bare. The
                      machine-readable value and the visible one come off the
                      same input, so they cannot disagree.
                    #}
                    {% if step.date %}
                        <time class="order-timeline__date" datetime="{{ step.date|date('Y-m-d') }}">
                            {{ step.date|date('d/m/Y') }}
                        </time>
                    {% endif %}
                </div>
            </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ol>
</div>
